PATCH 589: the base-name builder 21bd scans in 16-bit lengths; 18d1 unlinks the node 1345 found.

board:0014 (found by gate 586's wasm audio flows).  21bd (asm 0x21bd-0x222a) builds the 8-character
base name of the file name at DGROUP:0x740 into the owner record DGROUP:0x6b7e and releases the block
that record owns (18d1).  Patch 064 rebased the buffer but kept Ghidra's scan loops, which carry every
LENGTH as a `char *` -- `pcVar7 + (-1 - (int)pcVar10)`, compared against `(char *)0x9`, decremented to
`(char *)0x0`.  That is pointer arithmetic that never denotes an object; the wasm backend (-O2) folds
it: the `!= 0` bounds of the backward `\` scan and of the copy loop become `true`, the `\` scan runs
below the buffer until it meets a `\` byte anywhere in DGROUP, and the copy loop has no exit at all
(`loop { store8 ; br 0 } ; unreachable` in the emitted code).  On wasm this stayed invisible only
because the MGAVIDEO init's [0x550] (MGA 0197) rewrites 0x740 to BACKLAND.BIN first, the runaway scan
then yields a stem of 191 and the copy is skipped; native (-O0) copied "BACKLAND" -- a silent
native/wasm divergence in the owner record.  The moment [0x550] was not installed (the audio flows,
whose SOUNDDVR resize in 586 moved MGAVIDEO to 0x3e57 -- the shim's overlay lookup then resolved its
entry inside SOUNDDVR's stale range, re_out/fist_icall.c) the stem was 8 and the copy loop ran off the
end of memory: "RuntimeError: memory access out of bounds".

  The three scans are now what the asm does, in 16-bit offsets: `repnz scasb` for the NUL, the
  backward `\` scan (`std ; repnz scasb ; cld ; add di,2`) to the base-name start, the forward `.` scan
  to the stem end, `cmp cx,8 ; ja`, eight spaces (`mov ax,0x2020 ; stosw x4` -- Ghidra had turned the
  immediate into `&DAT_1000_e020`, the low 16 bits of a host address) and `rep movsb`.
  18d1 (asm 0x118d1): `call 1345 ; jb ; push ds ; mov ds,ax ; call 0d70 ; pop ds` -- 0d70 unlinks the
  node 1345 returned from the list whose header 1345 left in BP (g_fist_1345_bp); patch 065 handed it
  21bd's AX as the header pointer, and 21bd passed `&DAT_2000_2b78` (a host pointer) as the owner
  offset 0x6b78, so the search could never match.

